    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d583">

                <pb facs="0693" n="683" sameAs="frus:frus1948v08/pg683"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.50 Recovery/12–948: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Douglas</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-12-09T12:00:00+00:00">December 9,
                        1948—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 4:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5174. From Dickover.<anchor n="62" xml:id="d583-f1"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Discussed China situation with Dening<anchor n="63" xml:id="d583-f2"/> who
                        stated Chinese Ambassador<anchor n="64" xml:id="d583-f3"/> urged
                            Bevin<anchor n="65" xml:id="d583-f4"/> recently to use British influence
                        with US to have more aid sent to China. Foreign Office opinion, however, is
                        that no amount of aid which it would be possible to send China <pb
                            facs="0694" n="684" xml:id="pg684"/> now would alter situation
                        appreciably as rot in Nationalist Government too deep. Western powers now
                        must face fact that any government emerging from present chaos in China will
                        be entirely Communist or will be coalition which will soon be dominated by
                        Communists. Foreign Office, however, believes foreigners can continue do
                        business with such government. Many holes in Chinese economy (for example,
                        shortages of rice, cotton goods and petroleum) which Communists even with
                        Soviet help cannot fill. Therefore must rely on good will of Western powers.
                        For this reason Communists probably will not disturb foreigners or foreign
                        properties at least for present. Main thing at present is to keep foot in
                        door and consequently Foreign Office will retain British diplomatic and
                        consular officers at posts and is not urging essential British businessmen
                        to leave.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Foreign Office study on above subject plus section on SEA<anchor n="66"
                            xml:id="d583-f5"/> will probably be sent Washington next week for
                        delivery Department.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d583-f6"/> [Dickover.]</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Douglas</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d583-f1" n="62" xml:id="d583-f1-content">
                <p>Erie R. Dickover, Counselor of Embassy in the United Kingdom.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f2" n="63" xml:id="d583-f2-content">
                <p>Sir Maberly E. Dening, British Assistant Secretary of State for Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f3" n="64" xml:id="d583-f3-content">
                <p>Cheng Tien-hsi.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f4" n="65" xml:id="d583-f4-content">
                <p>Ernest Bevin, British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f5" n="66" xml:id="d583-f5-content">
                <p>Presumably Southeast Asia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f6" n="67" xml:id="d583-f6-content">
                <p>British study not received until January 5, 1949.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
